  "title": "Missing Number",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Cyclic sort",
  "patterns": [
    "cyclic sort",
    "array"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "blind-75"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "every value in the array, if it belongs anywhere, belongs at a specific index. what index would value v naturally sit at?",
    "try walking through the array and, for each position, swapping its value to the index equal to that value, repeating until the current position holds a value that already belongs there or is out of range",
    "after one pass of cyclic placement, scan through and find the first index i where nums[i] != i - that i is your missing number",
    "watch out for the value n itself, which has no valid index to be placed at since indices only go up to n-1"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "forgetting that the value n can never be placed at an index (indices only run 0..n-1), causing an infinite swap loop if not guarded",
    "using extra O(n) space with a hash set or boolean array when the problem expects O(1) space via placement",
    "off-by-one errors when comparing nums[i] to i versus i+1"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given an array containing n distinct numbers, each one taken from the range `[0, n]`. Exactly one number in that range is missing from the array.\n\n**Find and return the missing number.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= n <= 10^4",
      "0 <= nums[i] <= n",
      "all the numbers of nums are unique"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "the missing number is 0",
      "the missing number is n (the largest possible value)",
      "array of length 1"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
